<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaliakra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cape Kaliakra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaliakra is a long, narrow headland on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, near Kavarna, with cliffs up to 70 metres high. Fortified since antiquity, it was the capital of the 14th-century Dobrujan principality under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrotitsa">Dobrotitsa</a>. In 1791 a Russian fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-ushakov">Fyodor Ushakov</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> off the cape. The ruins of its fortress are now a nature reserve and tourist site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaliakra.jpg" alt="Kaliakra" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cabo_de_Kaliakra,_Bulgaria,_2016-05-27,_DD_134.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The headland lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-dobruja">Southern Dobruja</a> region, 12 kilometres east of Kavarna, 60 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> and 65 kilometres south-west of Mangalia. It sits on the Via Pontica, a major bird migration route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> into eastern and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its reserve shelters rare breeding birds such as the pied wheatear, saker falcon and lesser grey shrike, while dolphins and cormorants can be seen offshore. The name is Byzantine Greek, from words meaning beautiful and headland; its Thracian name was Tirizis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest settlement dates to the 4th century BCE, when the Thracian Tiris lived here. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> placed here the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, Alexander's general and ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. A second wall was built inland in the Hellenistic era, round towers were completed in 341 and 342, and in the later 4th century a third wall 2.90 metres thick went up still farther from the tip. In the 5th and 6th centuries the fortress was one of the 15 cities of the province of Scythia, and some researchers see a decline in the 7th century.</p><p>The site flourished in the second half of the 14th century as capital of the despotate under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balik">Balik</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrotitsa">Dobrotitsa</a>, whose town struck its own coins and whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, the first navy built by native rulers. The principality fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1393 and 1394. The Vlach voivode Mircea Stari held the surrounding lands briefly in 1402, and in 1444 the knights of King Vladislav III were stopped near the cape. In 1791 Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-ushakov">Fyodor Ushakov</a>'s squadron defeated Hussein Pasha's larger fleet offshore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Remains of the walls, the water supply, the baths and the prince's residence survive on the tip of the headland. The first lighthouse was built in 1866, and the present one in 1901. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> at the entrance, the Gate of the 40 Maidens, recalls the legend of forty Bulgarian girls who tied their hair together and leapt into the sea rather than be captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and a monument to Ushakov was raised in 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaliakra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Balik's brother Dobrotitsa (c. 1347–1386), styled despot by Byzantium, ruled from <b>Kaliakra</b> and Varna.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-dobruja">Despotate of Dobruja</a></li></ul></section>
